006],[-95.53024,39.22037],[-95.52976,39.22037],[-95.52953,39.22019],[-95.52948,39.21994],[-95.54078,39.1805],[-95.54906,39.16113],[-95.55793,39.14864],[-95.57178,39.1368],[-95.60003,39.11992]],"kind":"swath"},{"t":30,"ef":"EF2","ring":[[-95.42004,39.34989],[-95.4201,39.35008],[-95.36386,39.39509],[-95.32643,39.42702],[-95.28903,39.46079],[-95.2329,39.51336],[-95.12043,39.62024],[-95.12,39.62041],[-95.11962,39.62029],[-95.11947,39.62],[-95.11957,39.61976],[-95.2321,39.5129],[-95.2885,39.46044],[-95.32607,39.42674],[-95.36364,39.39491],[-95.42004,39.34989]],"kind":"swath"}],"story":["26 April 1991 was a major outbreak across Kansas and Oklahoma, and its worst tornado touched down south of Wichita, crossed Haysville and the south-east of the city past McConnell Air Force Base, and went on to Andover as an F5.","At the Golden Spur mobile home park in Andover the tornado killed thirteen people; seventeen died in all.","It was among the first tornadoes to be filmed by so many people from so many angles, and the day's video \u2014 including a television crew sheltering under a Kansas Turnpike overpass \u2014 put storm chasing on the evening news. That tape also spread a dangerous myth: an overpass is not a shelter, and people have since died believing it was.","The same afternoon, a portable Doppler radar from the University of Oklahoma measured some of the highest winds then recorded in a tornado near Red Rock, Oklahoma."],"facts":[{"label":"Deaths","value":"17","note":"13 at the Golden Spur"},{"label":"Rating","value":"F5"},{"label":"Overpass myth","value":"Started here","note":"Overpasses are not shelters"}]}